  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 252 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThe notable pattern shift is expected to be in full swing by \nThursday, as cooler than seasonal temperatures are expected into the \nearly portions of next week. Have seen a slight warming trend in the \nlatest forecast runs, which now indicate highs late week and through \nthe weekend will generally be in the upper 70s to lower 80s. Still, \neven though there is a slight warming trend in forecast highs, these \nstill remain cooler than seasonal, as typical highs this time of \nyear are still around 87 to 89 degrees. A closer examination at the \nprobabilistic temperature spreads show still a fair amount of \nuncertainty in exact temperatures through this pattern shift. Much \nwill largely depend on cloud cover and precipitation chances through \nthe week, though some questions still remain on how far south an \narea of cooler temperatures aloft will be able to track. If this \ncooler air can sink further south, then we may still favor highs \nstruggling to leave the 70s across most of the region. However, even \nwith this slight uncertainty, even the forecast highs in the 80s are \nonly a few degrees above the 80 degree mark. Interestingly enough, \nthese slightly warmer highs still remain between the 10th and 25th \npercentile compared to local climatology, giving higher confidence \nin cooler than average temperatures. \n\nWith the upper level trough remaining to the west, will also see \nsome enhancement in the lower level flow, which should bring a plume \nof monsoonal moisture across the region through the weekend. This \nplume of enhanced moisture will support higher than average PWAT \nvalues, supporting locally heavy rainfall across most of the region. \nWith this push of moisture and a fairly active weather pattern, \ndaily precipitation chances are expected across most of the region, \nwith potential for some beneficial rainfall totals through the \nweekend. Ensembles remain fairly split on their solutions for where \nheavier rain is likely to set up. Looking at likelihood of exceeding \none inch of total precipitation through Saturday evening, the \nEuropean solution indicates a 50 percent chance for most of the area \nnorth of I-80 getting at least an inch, with a 70 percent chance of \nthis occurring north of Highway 2. The GEFS solution meanwhile only \nindicates a 20 percent chance of this occurring across most of the \narea, and only a 40 percent chance north of Highway 20! Currently, \nensemble guidance continues this difference when reduced to even one \nhalf inch, with the European solution well over 50 percent north of \nI-80, and the GEFS only 30 to 40 percent north of I-80. At the \nleast, thinking that wetting rainfall will occur, however, question \nremains on what the higher end amounts will be through the weekend. \nFor what it is worth, WPC has issued a Marginal Risk for Excessive \nRainfall each day through Saturday for all of western and north \ncentral Nebraska.",
